WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.780
Open file descriptors and handles are part of the launch context.

2
00:00:03.880 --> 00:00:13.720
A child may inherit access to files, sockets, pipes, logs, temporary files, or other resources if the parent does not control inheritance.

3
00:00:13.820 --> 00:00:19.900
That access can affect confidentiality, integrity, cleanup, and resource lifetime.

4
00:00:20.180 --> 00:00:22.900
Children should inherit only the resources they need.

5
00:00:23.000 --> 00:00:27.240
Standard input, output, and error should be set deliberately.

6
00:00:27.340 --> 00:00:34.460
A helper may need a pipe for input, a pipe for output, a log destination, or no inherited standard input at all.

7
00:00:34.560 --> 00:00:38.800
Leaving streams unspecified can make behavior depend on the launcher environment.

8
00:00:39.080 --> 00:00:45.640
Close-on-exec or non-inheritable handle behavior helps reduce accidental leakage where the platform supports it.

9
00:00:45.740 --> 00:00:54.620
The important design point is that inheritable resources should be opt-in and reviewable, not incidental side effects of whatever the parent had open.

10
00:00:54.900 --> 00:00:57.540
Pipes and logs need cleanup plans.

11
00:00:57.640 --> 00:01:06.760
The parent should close unused ends, read or drain output according to the workflow, handle errors, and avoid leaving descriptors open longer than needed.

12
00:01:06.860 --> 00:01:12.940
Incorrect pipe handling can cause hangs, missing output, or resource leaks.

13
00:01:13.220 --> 00:01:16.260
Open files and sockets may carry sensitive access.

14
00:01:16.360 --> 00:01:22.120
A child with an inherited descriptor may not need to know a path or credentials to use the resource.

15
00:01:22.220 --> 00:01:28.620
That is why descriptor inheritance should be reviewed alongside identity, permissions, and the child's role.

16
00:01:28.900 --> 00:01:31.380
I/O redirection should be explicit.

17
00:01:31.480 --> 00:01:41.880
Reviewers should be able to see where child input comes from, where output goes, whether errors are captured, and how those streams are cleaned up on success, failure, and timeout.
